I am in love I talk about it all the  time I talk to my friends about it I  talk to my roommates about it I share  way too many details of strangers I show  them weird pictures I'm practically  shouting it from the rooftops I'm  totally and utterly in love with things  and buy things I mean the clothes you're  wearing the chair you're sitting in the  pen you're holding I mean everything you  touch and everything you see because to  me things aren't just things today I  want to share my love of things i want  to share why I think things are so  important to me and to you because  things have impacts that are far greater  than what we think of when we just think  of things things created our past they  made us who we are today they're shaping  our every moment in the present and  they're creating the future and the  building of things the act of physically  creating things has the power to  transform lives I feel like I'm working  in Sanford's product realization lab  this is a place where you can build  pretty much anything you can imagine you  can find every tool you could ever dream  up and you can do things like 3d  printing laser cutting injection molding  silversmithing this fall I tried welding  for the first time and I was completely  hooked so while many of my business  school classmates were spending their  time pursuing high-tech endeavors really  taking advantage of our location in  Silicon Valley at the GSB I spent night  after night doing something much more  low-tech dressed like this  so working in the product realization  lab has changed how I dress but more  than that it's changed how I look at the  world in many ways I was disconnected  from the things around me beings  magically appear on shelves and I bought  them took them home use them broke them  and they went back to the same stores  about new things that magically appeared  on the same shelves I was disconnected  from the materials from how they were  made and from what they meant for me but  when I'm welding or working in the  woodshop when I'm covered in sawdust or  steel shavings I think about these  things differently the more time I spend  building things the more I appreciate  them more I appreciate the world because  things aren't just things and things  have never been just things from the  first moment our distant ancestors chose  certain stones and pounded them with  other stones to create the first tools  these things have been influencing where  we live how we communicate who we talk  to and even our physical bodies research  indicates that the way the bones in our  hands are shaped today is a direct  result of the original tools we built so  we developed our opposable thumbs and  our metacarpal structure that has  strength and dexterity to reflect these  original tools and from this first  moment in time things have been creating  and defining societies cultures are  remembered by the things that they build  its things that create culture things  create fashion art architecture music  and technology things create the world  because things aren't just things and  today we're surrounded by so many things  that are impacting what we do how we  feel how we think and some are playing a  larger role in your experience right now  like your chair do you notice your chair  how does it feel is it the right height  is it the right amount of cushion is it  making you want to sit up straight or  kind of slouch  down how was that in turn affecting your  experience right now and how are all the  other things in this room impacting how  you feel right now and all of these  things were built by someone or  something somewhere the internet does a  great job of connecting us across the  world but these things they physically  connect us with the people who built  them on these desks were connected to  the people who extracted the oil to make  the laminate for the top to the steel  workers who extruded the steel tubing  for the legs the factory workers who  kept the production lines running the  crew on the ship who brought it to us  the interior designer in the architect  who chose this specific desk for the  specific room in this orientation  everything is a story of connections and  of people because things aren't just  things and this brings me to the future  the things we build help us grow in  advance and learn and evolve and just  like the things in our past and our  present create who we are today the  things in the present will in the things  in the future will influence where we go  and what we do it's hard to say exactly  how but what we do know is that we're  moving further and further from building  these things ourselves learning more and  more of our designing on screens  interacting in a virtual world we're  becoming disconnected from manufacturing  schools are dropping home in home  economics and shop classes and we're  losing the basic building skills that  generations before us had and there's so  many great things that happen in the  virtual world so much beautiful design  but today I want to ask you to not  forget about building in the physical  world because building things with your  hands has serious benefits it engages  your mind in a way that the virtual  world just cannot research shows that  when we move our hands to build things  our brains are flooded with dopamine and  serotonin these are two  neurotransmitters that are linked with  higher levels of these are linked with  lower depression and mood disorders and  when we move our hands rather than other  parts of our bodies we actually release  more dopamine and serotonin  there are other benefits that are less  measurable but still very real making  things engages both hemispheres of your  brain which can improve focus and  building things allows you to feel a  sense of usefulness in control in a  world that can often feel abstract and  out of control and if we do all of our  designing on screens and all of our  iterating and PowerPoint we run the risk  of losing this and we run the risk of  kids losing this too much time in front  of screens has been linked with obesity  irregular sleep and behavioral problems  in children on the other hand building  things has ties to higher student  engagement and graduation rates in  California students who took shop  classes graduated at higher rates they  performed better on high school exit  exams and they were more likely to  pursue a post-high school education the  National Science Foundation found that  students who studied engineering using  hands on building techniques learn more  than their peers who studied using books  and lectures and what other creative  programs such as art or cut from schools  these schools see increase of vandalism  in delinquency because building building  things allows kids to feel a sense of  control they learn how to try and fail  and then try again when glue won't hold  or a joint won't stick they learn how to  actually put a theory into practice and  they learn about persistence and  initiative which are two things that  studies show maybe more closely linked  to success than intelligence and they  get to come home from school and say I  made this I believe it is critical to  provide learning opportunities that are  not driven by a mouse or screen or an  imaginary world built from pixels I want  kids and us to see the world around us  to touch the world around us and to  create the world around us because  things aren't just things so the good  news is it's so close this world is  right at our fingertips maybe after  hearing me today you want to learn to  weld that is so exciting please come  with me come to the product realz  in lab or go to a tech shop or look  online for classes in your community and  you can start small too and still see  the effects of building you can write a  handwritten letter or make your own  jewelry or pottery or take a stab at  fixing that broken chair leg it's not  going to get any more broken and when  you sign your kids up for classes in  sports and music there might also be  programs that teach fashion design or  how to build robots so after today you  might not start welding you might miss  our building your own furniture or  making your own soap but I would invite  you to take a moment to look at the  things around you to touch the things  around you and to think about what they  mean for the world because I'm totally  in love with things because things  aren't just things
 

